<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>30-60-90 Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Find every missing side, the area, and the perimeter of a 30-60-90 triangle from a single known side.</p>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle is a right triangle whose three angles measure 30°, 60°, and 90°. It is called a special right triangle because its side lengths always follow the same fixed ratio, no matter how large or small the triangle is. The 30-60-90 triangle calculator on this page uses that ratio to find the short leg, the long leg, the hypotenuse, the area, and the perimeter from one side length that you enter.</p>
  <p>In triangle ABC, angle A is 30°, angle B is 60°, and angle C is 90°. Side BC is the short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, opposite the 30° angle. Side AC is the long leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, opposite the 60° angle. Side AB is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, opposite the 90° angle. The three sides always satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />, which means <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />.</p>
  <p>If <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2 \\times 5 = 10]]')} /> cm. The calculator can also work out the area and the perimeter from the same short leg, so one measurement is enough to describe the whole triangle. The sections below explain the ratio, the seven calculator modes, and the formulas, with worked examples at every step.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate Missing Sides, Area, and Perimeter</h2>
  <p>Seven modes cover every way to start from one known side.</p>
  <p>The calculator on this page has seven modes, one for each measurement it can find. Pick the mode that matches the side you know and the measurement you need. Each mode asks for one input only, because the fixed ratio fills in everything else. The table below lists all seven modes with the input each one needs and the result it returns.</p>
  <p>To use a mode, first work out which side you know. The side opposite the 30° angle is the short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, the side opposite the 60° angle is the long leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and the side opposite the 90° angle is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Enter that measurement as a positive number, pick a unit, and click the Calculate button. The result appears with step-by-step working, so you can see the formula and the substitution, not just the answer. Use Reset to clear the fields before starting a new problem.</p>
  <p>Different modes need different formulas because each one starts from a different known side. Finding the long leg from the short leg multiplies by √3, while finding the short leg from the hypotenuse divides by 2. The area and perimeter modes start from the short leg and calculate the other sides internally first, then apply the area or perimeter formula. You never need to work out the missing sides yourself before using those two modes.</p>
