<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a 30-60-90 Triangle?</h2>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle is named for its three angles.</p>
  <p>It is a right triangle, which means one of its angles is exactly 90°. The other two angles are 30° and 60°, and the name lists all three in order. Because the right angle is fixed and the other two angles are fixed as well, the shape of the triangle is fully determined: every 30-60-90 triangle has the same shape, and two such triangles differ only in size.</p>
  <p>It is called a special right triangle because its side lengths follow a fixed ratio. In most right triangles, knowing one side tells you nothing about the others. In a 30-60-90 triangle, knowing one side tells you all of them, because the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} /> locks the three sides together. That is why the calculator can find five measurements from a single input.</p>
  <p>The three angles add up to 180°, as they must in every triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[30° + 60° + 90° = 180°]]')} />. The triangle can be as small as a few millimeters or as large as a building plot, and the angles stay the same while the sides scale up together. The ratio stays the same too, so a triangle with short leg 5 cm and a triangle with short leg 5 m are the same shape at different sizes.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('thirty-60-90-main.svg')} alt="Labeled 30-60-90 triangle ABC with angles 30, 60, and 90 degrees, short leg a, long leg a times square root of 3, and hypotenuse 2a" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC: angles 30°, 60°, and 90°, with sides a, a√3, and 2a.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-sides -->
<section id="sec-sides" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Short Leg, Long Leg, and Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>The three sides take their names from the angles opposite them.</p>
  <p>The short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the side opposite the 30° angle. It is the shortest side of the triangle, because the smallest angle always sits opposite the shortest side. In triangle ABC it is side BC. The short leg is the natural starting point for most calculations, because the other two sides are simple multiples of it: the long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The long leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the side opposite the 60° angle. It is longer than the short leg and shorter than the hypotenuse. In triangle ABC it is side AC. Its length is <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732]]')} /> times the short leg, so it is a little under twice as long. The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the side opposite the 90° angle, side AB, and it is the longest side of the triangle, exactly twice the short leg.</p>
  <p>Identifying sides by their opposite angles is more reliable than judging by how the diagram is drawn. A triangle can be rotated or flipped, so the short leg might appear on the left, on the right, or at the bottom. The opposite-angle rule never changes: find the 30° angle, and the side across from it is the short leg. Find the 90° angle, marked with a small square, and the side across from it is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Opposite angle</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Size</th></tr>
