<!-- Section 5: sec-ratio -->
<section id="sec-ratio" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>The 30-60-90 Triangle Side Ratio</h2>
  <p>The ratio 1:√3:2 fixes every 30-60-90 triangle.</p>
  <p>The side ratio of a 30-60-90 triangle is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a : b : c = 1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Each part of the ratio describes one side. The short leg represents one unit, the long leg represents √3 units, and the hypotenuse represents two units. Written with the short leg as <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, the three sides are <span set:html={rich('[[a = a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />. If the short leg is 1 cm, the long leg is √3 cm and the hypotenuse is 2 cm. If the short leg is 10 cm, the long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[10\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm and the hypotenuse is 20 cm.</p>
  <p>The ratio stays the same regardless of the size of the triangle, because all 30-60-90 triangles are similar. Scaling a triangle up or down multiplies every side by the same factor, so the proportions between the sides do not change. Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4]]')} /> cm as an example: the long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4\\sqrt{3} \\approx 6.928]]')} /> cm and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 8]]')} /> cm, and the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[4 : 6.928 : 8]]')} /> is the same as <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Keep the exact radical form whenever you can. √3 is approximately 1.732, but it is an irrational number, so no decimal equals it exactly. Writing <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm is exact, while 6.928 cm is a rounded approximation. Use the exact form in your working and in final answers when the question asks for an exact value, and give the decimal only when an approximate answer is needed.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('thirty-60-90-ratio.svg')} alt="Three bars comparing the short leg, long leg, and hypotenuse in the ratio 1 to square root of 3 to 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The fixed ratio 1:√3:2 compares the short leg, the long leg, and the hypotenuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-why-sqrt3 -->
<section id="sec-why-sqrt3" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Square Root of 3 Appear?</h2>
  <p>The square root of 3 comes from cutting an equilateral triangle in half.</p>
  <p>Start with an equilateral triangle whose side length is <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />. All three sides are equal and all three angles are 60°. Now draw an altitude from one vertex to the opposite side. The altitude is the line from the vertex that meets the opposite side at a right angle.</p>
  <p>The altitude bisects the base, splitting it into two equal segments of length <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. It also divides the equilateral triangle into two congruent right triangles. Each smaller triangle has angles 30°, 60°, and 90°: the 60° angle survives at the base, the altitude creates a 90° angle where it meets the base, and the 60° angle at the top is split into two 30° angles. The hypotenuse of each small triangle is the side of the equilateral triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />, and the short leg is half the base, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The remaining leg of the small triangle is the altitude itself. Call it <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and apply the Pythagorean theorem:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = (2a)^2 - a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = 4a^2 - a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = 3a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The 3 inside the square root comes from <span set:html={rich('[[4a^2 - a^2 = 3a^2]]')} />, the difference between the square of the hypotenuse and the square of the short leg. That is the whole reason √3 appears in every 30-60-90 formula: the long leg is the altitude of an equilateral triangle, and Pythagoras forces it to be <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. The derivation also proves the side ratio, because the three sides of the small triangle are now <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('thirty-60-90-equilateral.svg')} alt="Equilateral triangle of side 2a split by an altitude into two congruent 30-60-90 triangles with altitude a times square root of 3" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">An altitude splits an equilateral triangle of side 2a into two 30-60-90 triangles.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-long-leg -->
<section id="sec-long-leg" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Long Leg From the Short Leg</h2>
  <p>Multiply the short leg by √3 to get the long leg.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the long leg and <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the short leg. The formula is a direct reading of the side ratio: the long leg represents √3 units for every 1 unit of short leg, so the long leg is √3 times the short leg. Multiplication is required because the ratio is a scaling relationship, and √3 appears because the long leg is the altitude of the equilateral triangle that generates this triangle.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm as an example:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = 5\\sqrt{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b \\approx 8.660 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is longer than the short leg, as it must be: √3 is about 1.732, so multiplying by it increases the length. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm, and 8.660 cm is the decimal approximation. Keep the exact form when the question asks for it, and round the decimal only at the end of your working, never in the middle.</p>
  <p>Use this mode whenever the short leg is the known side and the long leg is the unknown one. In a textbook problem, the short leg is usually the side opposite the 30° angle, so check the angle before you multiply. A quick sense check helps: the long leg should come out a little under twice the short leg, since √3 is a little under 2. If your answer is more than twice the short leg, you have multiplied by the wrong number.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('thirty-60-90-long-leg.svg')} alt="30-60-90 triangle with short leg 5 cm and long leg labeled 5 times square root of 3, about 8.660 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Long leg from short leg: a = 5 cm gives b = 5√3 ≈ 8.660 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-hypotenuse -->
<section id="sec-hypotenuse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Hypotenuse From the Short Leg</h2>
  <p>Double the short leg to get the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 2a]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse and <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the short leg. The hypotenuse is always exactly twice the short leg, with no square root involved. This is the simplest of the 30-60-90 formulas, and it is also the most useful for quick mental checks.</p>
  <p>The reason goes back to the equilateral triangle. The hypotenuse of the 30-60-90 triangle is one full side of the equilateral triangle, while the short leg is half of a side, because the altitude bisects the base. A full side is twice a half side, so the hypotenuse is twice the short leg. Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 2 \\times 7]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 14 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The answer is exact, with no rounding, because doubling a whole number gives a whole number. If the short leg is a decimal, the hypotenuse is still exactly twice it, so <span set:html={rich('[[a = 2.5]]')} /> cm gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>This mode is the one to pick when a problem gives the short leg and asks for the longest side. Because the answer is a simple doubling, it is also the fastest way to spot a wrong short leg: if the hypotenuse in the problem is not twice your short leg, either the short leg is wrong or the triangle is not a 30-60-90 triangle. Check the angles before trusting the doubling.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('thirty-60-90-hypotenuse.svg')} alt="30-60-90 triangle with short leg 5 cm and hypotenuse labeled 2a equals 10 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Hypotenuse from short leg: c = 2a, so a = 5 cm gives c = 10 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-short-leg-hyp -->
<section id="sec-short-leg-hyp" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Short Leg From the Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Halve the hypotenuse to get the short leg.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{c}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the short leg and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. This formula is the reverse of <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />: since the hypotenuse is twice the short leg, the short leg is half the hypotenuse. Rearranging <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} /> by dividing both sides by 2 gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[c = 18]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{18}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = 9 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The answer is exact, with no square root and no rounding. This relationship applies specifically to 30-60-90 triangles. In a general right triangle, the hypotenuse is not twice the short leg, so do not use this formula unless you know the angles are 30°, 60°, and 90°. If the hypotenuse is an odd number, the short leg is a half value, for example <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} /> cm gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7.5]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>This mode is the starting point for most problems that give the hypotenuse. Once the short leg is known, the long leg follows from <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> and the area and perimeter follow from their own formulas. A half-value answer is normal here and is not a sign of an error, so do not round 7.5 cm to 8 cm: the exact half is the correct answer.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-short-leg-long -->
<section id="sec-short-leg-long" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Short Leg From the Long Leg</h2>
  <p>Divide the long leg by √3 to get the short leg.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{3}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the short leg and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the long leg. This formula comes from rearranging <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />: dividing both sides by √3 isolates <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. Dividing by √3 reverses the original multiplication, so it undoes exactly what the forward formula did.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{3}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Rationalize the denominator by multiplying the top and bottom by √3: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{3} = 4\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm, which is approximately 6.928 cm. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm, and 6.928 cm is the decimal form. The short leg is smaller than the long leg, as it must be, since dividing by a number larger than 1 shrinks the value.</p>
  <p>Rationalizing is worth doing because it leaves the answer in the standard exact form, a whole number times √3. A decimal approximation hides that structure, and an un-rationalized fraction with √3 in the denominator is not the form exam answers expect. When the long leg is already a multiple of √3, the division is clean: <span set:html={rich('[[b = 9\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-long-leg-hyp -->
<section id="sec-long-leg-hyp" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Long Leg From the Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Multiply half the hypotenuse by √3 to get the long leg.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = \\frac{c\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the long leg and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. This formula is built from two steps. First find the short leg with <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, then find the long leg with <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. Substituting the first formula into the second gives <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\frac{c}{2}\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, which is the same as <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\frac{c\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[c = 16]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = \\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = 8\\sqrt{3} \\approx 13.856 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result sits between the short leg and the hypotenuse, as it should. The short leg here is <span set:html={rich('[[a = 8]]')} /> cm, the long leg is about 13.856 cm, and the hypotenuse is 16 cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[8 < 13.856 < 16]]')} />. That ordering is a quick check on any answer from this formula.</p>
  <p>Doing the calculation in two steps is a good habit even though the single formula does both at once. Find the short leg first, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{2} = 8]]')} /> cm, then multiply by √3. If the two-step route and the single formula disagree, the error is in the arithmetic, not the formula, and the two-step route shows exactly where it happened.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-area -->
<section id="sec-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a 30-60-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>The area needs only the short leg, because the long leg follows from it.</p>
  <p>The area of any right triangle is half the product of its two legs, because the legs act as the base and the perpendicular height:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, substitute to get the area from the short leg alone:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{a^2\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Division by two is necessary because a triangle covers half the rectangle formed by its two legs. The final area is expressed in square units, such as square centimeters, because area measures surface, not length. Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm. First find the long leg, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 6\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm, then calculate:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{6 \\times 6\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = 18\\sqrt{3} \\approx 31.177 \\text{ cm}^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The calculator's area mode accepts the short leg and calculates the long leg internally, so you enter only <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and the calculator does the rest. If you know the long leg instead of the short leg, use the <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">right triangle area calculator</a>, which accepts both legs directly.</p>
  <p>Area grows with the square of the short leg, so doubling the short leg quadruples the area. A triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 12]]')} /> cm has four times the area of the <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm triangle, about 124.708 cm², not twice it. That square relationship is easy to forget, so check it whenever a problem scales a triangle up or down.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('thirty-60-90-area.svg')} alt="30-60-90 triangle with short leg 6 cm and long leg 6 times square root of 3 cm as base and height, area 18 times square root of 3 square cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Area from the short leg: a = 6 cm gives A = 18√3 ≈ 31.177 cm².</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-perimeter -->
<section id="sec-perimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Perimeter of a 30-60-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Add all three sides, then factor out the short leg.</p>
  <p>The perimeter is the total length around the triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a + b + c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute the special triangle relationships, <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a + a\\sqrt{3} + 2a]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Combine the like terms to get the simplified form:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a(3 + \\sqrt{3})]]]')} /></p>
  <p>All three side lengths are included, because the perimeter traces the full boundary of the triangle: down the short leg, across the long leg, and back along the hypotenuse. Leaving out any side gives a shorter distance that is not the perimeter. Perimeter is a linear measurement, not an area measurement, so the answer is in plain units such as centimeters, never square centimeters.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 5(3 + \\sqrt{3})]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P \\approx 23.660 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The exact form is <span set:html={rich('[[5(3 + \\sqrt{3})]]')} /> cm, and 23.660 cm is the decimal form. The calculator's perimeter mode accepts the short leg and adds the three sides internally. If you already know all three sides, the <a href="/right-triangle-perimeter-calculator/">right triangle perimeter calculator</a> adds them directly.</p>
  <p>Perimeter grows in direct proportion to the short leg, so doubling the short leg doubles the perimeter. A triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 10]]')} /> cm has a perimeter of about 47.321 cm, exactly twice the 23.660 cm of the <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm triangle. That linear scaling is a useful check: if doubling the short leg does not double your perimeter, the arithmetic went wrong somewhere.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('thirty-60-90-perimeter.svg')} alt="30-60-90 triangle with all three sides labeled 5 cm, 5 times square root of 3 cm, and 10 cm, perimeter about 23.660 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Perimeter from the short leg: a = 5 cm gives P = 5(3 + √3) ≈ 23.660 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Solving a 30-60-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>One triangle, a = 5 cm, worked through from start to finish.</p>
  <p>Start with the short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm and find every other measurement of the triangle, one formula at a time. Each step uses a formula from the sections above, and the summary table at the end collects the results.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the long leg: <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3} = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm.</li>
    <li>Find the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a = 2 \\times 5 = 10]]')} /> cm.</li>
    <li>Find the area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{ab}{2} = \\frac{5 \\times 5\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\approx 21.651]]')} /> square centimeters.</li>
    <li>Find the perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a + b + c = 5 + 5\\sqrt{3} + 10 = 15 + 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 23.660]]')} /> cm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>All four measurements describe the same triangle. The sides <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[8.660]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[10]]')} /> satisfy the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} /> and the Pythagorean theorem, <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + (5\\sqrt{3})^2 = 25 + 75 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />. The area and the perimeter are two different kinds of measurement of that same triangle: the area covers its surface, while the perimeter traces its boundary.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Measurement</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Exact value</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Approximate value</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Short leg a</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Long leg b</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5√3 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">8.660 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">10 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">10 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Area A</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">25√3/2 cm²</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">21.651 cm²</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Perimeter P</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">15 + 5√3 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">23.660 cm</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of 30-60-90 Triangle Calculations</h2>
  <p>Eight worked examples, one for each calculator mode plus a larger case.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Find the Long Leg</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[b \\approx 6.928]]')} /> cm. The long leg is about 1.732 times the short leg, so 4 cm becomes about 6.928 cm. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Find the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2 \\times 9]]')} />. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 18]]')} /> cm. The hypotenuse is exactly twice the short leg, so the answer needs no rounding. This is the fastest check in the whole topic: any hypotenuse that is not twice the short leg signals an error.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Find the Short Leg From the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 20]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{20}{2}]]')} />. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 10]]')} /> cm. Halving the hypotenuse reverses the doubling in Example 2. The short leg is exactly half the hypotenuse, so the answer is exact.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Find the Short Leg From the Long Leg</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 15]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{3}}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{3}}]]')} />. Rationalizing gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm, so the result is <span set:html={rich('[[a \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm. Dividing by √3 shrinks the long leg back to the short leg, which is the reverse of multiplying by √3.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Find the Long Leg From the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 12]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\frac{c\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[b = 6\\sqrt{3} \\approx 10.392]]')} /> cm. The long leg sits between the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm, and the hypotenuse, 12 cm, which confirms the ordering <span set:html={rich('[[a < b < c]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Calculate the Area</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 8]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{64\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 32\\sqrt{3} \\approx 55.426]]')} /> square centimeters. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[32\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm², and the decimal is rounded to three places. Area is in square units, unlike the side lengths.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Calculate the Perimeter</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(3 + \\sqrt{3})]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 3(3 + \\sqrt{3})]]')} />. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 9 + 3\\sqrt{3} \\approx 14.196]]')} /> cm. Adding the sides one by one gives the same result: <span set:html={rich('[[3 + 3\\sqrt{3} + 6 = 9 + 3\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm. Perimeter is in plain units, not square units.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Larger Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 10]]')} /> m. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[b = 10\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2 \\times 10]]')} /> m. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[b \\approx 17.321]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 20]]')} /> m. The ratio works at any scale: this triangle is the <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm triangle doubled, so every side is twice as long and the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} /> is unchanged.</p>
  <p>Exact answers and decimal approximations serve different purposes. The exact form, such as <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm, is precise and is what exam answers usually require. The decimal form, such as 6.928 cm, is easier to picture and to measure with a ruler. Give the exact form first, then the decimal, and round the decimal only at the final step.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-derive-pythagoras -->
<section id="sec-derive-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Derive the 30-60-90 Triangle Ratio Using Pythagoras</h2>
  <p>The ratio follows from Pythagoras plus one fact: the hypotenuse is twice the short leg.</p>
  <p>Let <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> be the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> the long leg, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> the hypotenuse. From the equilateral triangle construction, the hypotenuse is twice the short leg:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 2a]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now apply the Pythagorean theorem, which holds for every right triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = 4a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Rearrange by subtracting <span set:html={rich('[[a^2]]')} /> from both sides:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = 3a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take the square root of both sides. Lengths are positive, so the negative root is discarded:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The three sides are now <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />, which gives the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />. Every side-length formula on this page comes from this fixed relationship: the long-leg formula reads it directly, the hypotenuse formula is the starting fact, and the area and perimeter formulas substitute the two side expressions. The <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a> checks the same relationship for any right triangle.</p>
  <p>The proof needs only two ingredients, and both are easy to state. The first is the fact <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />, which comes from the equilateral triangle. The second is the Pythagorean theorem, which holds for every right triangle. Nothing else is assumed, so the proof shows that the ratio is forced by those two facts alone, not by a special case or a coincidence.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-equilateral -->
<section id="sec-equilateral" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between 30-60-90 Triangles and Equilateral Triangles</h2>
  <p>Every 30-60-90 triangle is half of an equilateral triangle.</p>
  <p>An equilateral triangle has three equal sides and three equal angles of 60°. Draw an altitude from one vertex to the opposite side, and the altitude bisects the base into two equal halves. It also splits the 60° angle at the top vertex into two 30° angles. The result is two congruent right triangles, each with angles 30°, 60°, and 90°.</p>
  <p>Each half keeps one full side of the equilateral triangle as its hypotenuse, takes half a side as its short leg, and uses the altitude as its long leg. That is why the hypotenuse is twice the short leg and why the long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. The √3 relationship appears because the altitude of an equilateral triangle is always <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} /> times the side length, which is the same fact written with different letters.</p>
  <p>This construction is useful in geometry and trigonometry because it turns the equilateral triangle into a source of exact values. The altitude of an equilateral triangle of side 10 cm is <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm, found by treating half of it as a 30-60-90 triangle with short leg 5 cm. The same construction gives the exact sine, cosine, and tangent values for 30° and 60°, which is why those values appear in every trigonometry table.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-trig -->
<section id="sec-trig" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Trigonometric Relationships in a 30-60-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>The fixed ratio gives exact sine, cosine, and tangent values for 30° and 60°.</p>
  <p>Trigonometric ratios compare sides of a right triangle. Sine is opposite over hypotenuse, cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, and tangent is opposite over adjacent. Because the sides of a 30-60-90 triangle are always in the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />, those comparisons always give the same values, whatever the size of the triangle.</p>
  <p>For the 30° angle, the opposite side is the short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and the adjacent side is the long leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin 30° = \\frac{a}{2a} = \\frac{1}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos 30° = \\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{2a} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan 30° = \\frac{a}{a\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}]]')} />. For the 60° angle, the opposite and adjacent sides swap, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin 60° = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos 60° = \\frac{1}{2}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan 60° = \\sqrt{3}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Notice the symmetry: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin 30° = \\cos 60°]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos 30° = \\sin 60°]]')} />, because the two acute angles are complements that add to 90°. These values agree with the fixed side ratio exactly, which is a good cross-check: if a sine value does not match the ratio, one of the two is wrong. Keep this section as background: the calculator on this page finds sides, area, and perimeter, and does not calculate trigonometric functions as a mode.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Angle</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">sin</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">cos</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">tan</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1/2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">√3/2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1/√3</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">60°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">√3/2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1/2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">√3</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your 30-60-90 Triangle Calculations</h2>
  <p>Four quick checks catch nearly every error.</p>
  <p>Run at least one check on every answer, and all four when the problem matters. The example below uses the triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm throughout.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Side Ratio</h3>
  <p>Divide the long leg by the short leg and the hypotenuse by the short leg: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{b}{a} = \\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{a} = 2]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{8.660}{5} \\approx 1.732]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{10}{5} = 2]]')} />, so the ratio holds. If either division gives a different value, one of the sides is wrong.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + (5\\sqrt{3})^2 = 25 + 75 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />, so the theorem holds. This check catches swapped legs, because using the wrong value for <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> breaks the equality. The <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a> runs this check for any three side lengths.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Angle Sum</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[30° + 60° + 90° = 180°]]')} />. This check is about the problem setup rather than the arithmetic: if the angles of your triangle do not add to 180°, or if they are not 30°, 60°, and 90°, the 30-60-90 formulas do not apply at all, and no side length from them can be trusted.</p>
  <h3>Check 4: Side Ordering</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a < b < c]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[5 < 8.660 < 10]]')} />, so the ordering holds. The short leg must be the smallest side, the long leg must sit in the middle, and the hypotenuse must be the longest. Any answer that breaks this order, such as a long leg longer than the hypotenuse, is wrong on its face.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of 30-60-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>Ten properties follow from the fixed ratio.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The triangle always has angles of 30°, 60°, and 90°, and those angles always add to 180°.</li>
    <li>The short leg is opposite the 30° angle, and it is the smallest side of the triangle.</li>
    <li>The long leg is opposite the 60° angle, and it sits between the short leg and the hypotenuse in length.</li>
    <li>The hypotenuse is opposite the 90° angle, and it is the longest side of the triangle.</li>
    <li>The hypotenuse is twice the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />, with no square root involved.</li>
    <li>The long leg is √3 times the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The side ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} /> is independent of the size of the triangle, so scaling the triangle leaves the ratio unchanged.</li>
    <li>All 30-60-90 triangles are similar, because they have the same three angles and the same side proportions.</li>
    <li>The area depends on the square of the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} />, so doubling the short leg quadruples the area.</li>
    <li>The perimeter scales directly with the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(3 + \\sqrt{3})]]')} />, so doubling the short leg doubles the perimeter.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- Section 21: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Solving 30-60-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>Fourteen errors show up again and again in 30-60-90 problems.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Mixing up the short and long legs: the short leg is opposite 30° and the long leg is opposite 60°. Identify each side by its opposite angle, not by how the diagram is rotated. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> opposite 30°, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> opposite 60°.</li>
    <li>Using √2 instead of √3: √2 belongs to the 45-45-90 triangle, whose ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} />. A 30-60-90 triangle always uses √3. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Multiplying the short leg by 2 to find the long leg: doubling gives the hypotenuse, not the long leg. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, doubling gives 10 cm, which is <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, while the long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Using <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> as the hypotenuse: the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm is the long leg, and the hypotenuse is 10 cm. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that the hypotenuse equals <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />: students sometimes apply Pythagoras to find the hypotenuse when doubling would do. Doubling is faster and exact. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Dividing by 2 instead of √3 when finding the short leg from the long leg: dividing <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> by 2 gives <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{b}{2}]]')} />, which is not the short leg. For <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm, dividing by 2 gives 6 cm, while the correct short leg is <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{3} \\approx 6.928]]')} /> cm. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{3}}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to divide by two when calculating area: the area is half the product of the legs. Using <span set:html={rich('[[A = ab]]')} /> doubles the true area. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[ab = 36\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm² is twice the correct <span set:html={rich('[[18\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm². Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Adding only two sides when calculating perimeter: the perimeter needs all three sides. Adding just the two legs for <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm gives <span set:html={rich('[[5 + 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 13.660]]')} /> cm, while the true perimeter is about 23.660 cm. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a + b + c]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Confusing exact answers with decimal approximations: <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm is exact, while 8.660 cm is rounded. Writing 8.660 cm as if it were exact loses the precision the radical keeps. Correct: give the exact form first, then the decimal.</li>
    <li>Rounding √3 too early: using 1.73 for √3 in the middle of a calculation shifts every later step. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[5 \\times 1.73 = 8.65]]')} /> cm instead of 8.660 cm. Correct: keep √3 exact through the working and round only the final answer.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units: a short leg in centimeters and a hypotenuse in meters cannot be combined directly. Convert every measurement to one unit before calculating. Correct: 0.5 m equals 50 cm, then calculate in centimeters.</li>
    <li>Applying the formulas to a triangle with different angles: the formulas <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} /> hold only when the angles are 30°, 60°, and 90°. A right triangle with angles 40° and 50° has a different ratio. Correct: confirm the angles first, then use the formulas.</li>
    <li>Confusing the 30-degree and 60-degree opposite sides: the side opposite 30° is the short leg and the side opposite 60° is the long leg, never the reverse. Swapping them makes the long leg shorter than the short leg, which is impossible. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> opposite 30°, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> opposite 60°.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that area requires square units: an area of 31.177 cm is wrong by unit, even if the number is right. Area measures surface, so it is 31.177 square centimeters. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 18\\sqrt{3} \\approx 31.177]]')} /> cm².</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 22: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of 30-60-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>The ratio turns up wherever a 30° or 60° angle meets a right angle.</p>
  <p>The most common use is geometry homework and trigonometry exercises, where 30-60-90 problems test whether students can identify the short leg, apply the ratio, and keep exact radical answers. Equilateral triangle height calculations use the same idea in reverse: the altitude of an equilateral triangle is the long leg of the 30-60-90 triangle formed by cutting it in half, so a side of 10 cm gives an altitude of <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Technical drawing, architecture, and construction geometry use 30-60-90 relationships when a design calls for a 30° or 60° angle, such as a roof slope, a ramp, or a diagonal brace. A builder who knows one side of such a triangle can find the others without measuring them directly. Classroom demonstrations use the triangle to show how a fixed ratio turns one measurement into a full description of a shape.</p>
  <p>Mathematical modeling uses the ratio wherever diagonal and height relationships in geometric shapes need exact values, for example in hexagonal patterns, where the 60° angles of a regular hexagon create 30-60-90 triangles inside the shape. These are realistic uses of the geometry itself, not claims about engineering tolerances: real design work adds material limits and safety margins beyond the exact geometric values on this page.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 23: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Work all six problems on paper before reading the answers.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Find the Long Leg From the Short Leg</h3>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm. Find the long leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Find the Hypotenuse From the Short Leg</h3>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of <span set:html={rich('[[a = 11]]')} /> cm. Find the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Find the Short Leg From the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle has a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c = 24]]')} /> cm. Find the short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Find the Long Leg From the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle has a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c = 14]]')} /> cm. Find the long leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Calculate the Area From the Short Leg</h3>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm. Find the area <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Calculate the Perimeter From the Short Leg</h3>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7]]')} /> cm. Find the perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3} \\approx 10.392]]')} /> cm. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[6\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 2: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a = 2 \\times 11 = 22]]')} /> cm. The hypotenuse is exactly twice the short leg.</p>
  <p>Answer 3: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{2} = \\frac{24}{2} = 12]]')} /> cm. The short leg is exactly half the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>Answer 4: <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\frac{c\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 7\\sqrt{3} \\approx 12.124]]')} /> cm. Check the ordering: the short leg is 7 cm, the long leg is about 12.124 cm, and the hypotenuse is 14 cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 5: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{81\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\approx 70.148]]')} /> square centimeters. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{81\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} /> cm².</p>
  <p>Answer 6: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(3 + \\sqrt{3}) = 7(3 + \\sqrt{3}) \\approx 33.124]]')} /> cm. Adding the sides one by one gives the same result: <span set:html={rich('[[7 + 7\\sqrt{3} + 14 = 21 + 7\\sqrt{3} \\approx 33.124]]')} /> cm.</p>
</section>

